<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Why we built Zenly</h2>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    A plain calendar can tell you when one person is free. It can't tell you when a provider, a room, and a piece of equipment are all free at the same time&mdash;and that's the scheduling problem most service businesses actually have. We built Zenly to model availability as it really works, not as a single grid of open and busy slots.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-4 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">The problem</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Real-world availability is never just one calendar&mdash;providers, rooms, and equipment each carry their own constraints, and double-bookings were being resolved by hand, back and forth, one message at a time. Without a timely nudge, booked slots quietly went empty.
  </p>
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50 p-6">
    <p class="mb-2 font-medium text-[#111827]">Where manual scheduling breaks down:</p>
    <ul class="space-y-2 text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">
      <li>&bull; Providers, locations, and equipment each carry constraints a single calendar can't represent</li>
      <li>&bull; Double-bookings get resolved by back-and-forth messages instead of automatically</li>
      <li>&bull; Reminders that go out too early or too late don't stop no-shows</li>
      <li>&bull; Every custom scheduling rule (buffers, combos, specialties) has to be applied by hand</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-4 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">How we built it</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    We modeled availability as a constraint problem instead of a single calendar: providers, locations, equipment, and custom rules all feed into one engine that computes real openings and resolves conflicts by proposing alternatives or rebooking automatically with approval. On top of that we layered AI-timed, channel-aware reminders tuned to when a given customer is actually likely to respond.
  </p>
  <div class="space-y-3">
    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-navy-100/60 bg-white p-5 shadow-subtle">
      <p class="mb-1 font-medium text-[#111827]">Availability as a constraint problem</p>
      <p class="text-[13.5px] text-[#6B7280]">Providers, locations, equipment, and custom rules all feed one engine that computes real openings, instead of checking calendars one at a time.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-navy-100/60 bg-white p-5 shadow-subtle">
      <p class="mb-1 font-medium text-[#111827]">Automatic conflict resolution</p>
      <p class="text-[13.5px] text-[#6B7280]">When two bookings collide, Zenly proposes alternatives, manages the waitlist, and can rebook automatically once the customer approves&mdash;no back-and-forth needed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-navy-100/60 bg-white p-5 shadow-subtle">
      <p class="mb-1 font-medium text-[#111827]">Reminders timed to behavior, not a fixed schedule</p>
      <p class="text-[13.5px] text-[#6B7280]">Reminders go out via SMS, email, or voice at the moment a given customer is actually likely to respond, and make rescheduling as easy as confirming.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-navy-100/60 bg-white p-5 shadow-subtle">
      <p class="mb-1 font-medium text-[#111827]">Connects to calendars already in use</p>
      <p class="text-[13.5px] text-[#6B7280]">Zenly syncs with Google Calendar, Outlook, and Apple Calendar, and embeds anywhere through a JavaScript SDK.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

